// Lesson 1.4a — Movement I: Input, Direction, Vector
// Core build lab: breaking down movement into input -> vector -> translate.
// Reading Unity input functions (GetAxis vs GetAxisRaw vs GetKey),
// comparing use cases, and building movement from scratch in PlayerController.cs.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.4a — Movement I: Input, Direction, Vector</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    In video games, movement looks natural and effortless. In computer science,
    it is an algorithm built by breaking a complex behavior into three smaller problems:
    <strong>read player input → calculate a motion vector → translate position</strong>.
    Today, you read Unity's input functions and write the foundational movement code from scratch.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Before writing code, computer scientists read the documentation for the tools
        already provided by the engine. Unity gives us several different ways to detect
        when a player touches the keyboard or controller. Look at these three functions:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Unity Function</th><th>Return Type</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Input.GetAxis("Horizontal")</code></td><td><code>float</code> (-1.0 to 1.0)</td><td>Smooth ramp: gradually accelerates toward -1 or +1, then coasts back to 0.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Input.GetAxisRaw("Horizontal")</code></td><td><code>float</code> (-1, 0, or 1)</td><td>Instant snap: returns exactly -1, 0, or 1 immediately with no smoothing filter.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Input.GetKey(KeyCode.D)</code></td><td><code>bool</code> (true or false)</td><td>Binary state: true while the physical key 'D' is held down; false otherwise.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Evaluating Multiple Ways to Solve a Problem</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If you are building a driving game or a character with heavy momentum, which function (<code>GetAxis</code> or <code>GetAxisRaw</code>) gives the better feel, and why?</li>
            <li>If you are building a precision grid-based puzzle game or a fast arcade game where you must stop on a dime, why might <code>GetAxisRaw</code> be superior to <code>GetAxis</code>?</li>
            <li>What happens if a player wants to play with Arrow Keys or a USB game controller instead of WASD? Why is querying a virtual axis like <code>"Horizontal"</code> more flexible than hardcoding <code>KeyCode.D</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Do not look for a complete movement script to copy. In real software engineering, you read
        how individual functions operate in isolation, understand their inputs and outputs, and assemble
        them into your own custom architecture. Look at these isolated snippets from three completely different machines:
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — Polling an Axis on a Warehouse Forklift:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Reads virtual axis data (-1.0 to +1.0) based on player keys or joystick tilt
float steeringIntent = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — Constructing a 2D Vector for a Freight Elevator:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Constructs a Vector2 representing 0 horizontal motion and frame-scaled vertical displacement
Vector2 verticalStep = new Vector2(0, liftSpeed * Time.deltaTime);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — Translating Space on an Airport Conveyor Belt:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Directly displaces the Transform's coordinates by the provided vector
transform.Translate(beltDisplacementVector);</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If <code>steeringIntent</code> is <code>-1.0</code> (player pressing A) and <code>liftSpeed</code> is <code>4.0</code>, how many units does <code>liftSpeed * Time.deltaTime</code> travel across 1 second of accumulated frames?</li>
            <li>In the elevator example, why is X set to <code>0</code>? What would happen if X also had a non-zero value?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Your Engineering Mission: Build 2D Movement in <code>PlayerController.cs</code>.</strong>
        Open <code>Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs</code>. You will construct a full two-axis movement pipeline
        from scratch by solving three modular sub-problems.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>PlayerController.cs</code>:</strong>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <strong>Variables &amp; Tuning Knobs:</strong>
                At the top of the class, declare:
                <ul>
                    <li>Two serialized private float fields: <code>xSpeed</code> (default <code>5.0f</code>) and <code>ySpeed</code> (default <code>5.0f</code>).</li>
                    <li>Two private float fields to store raw axis input: <code>xDirection</code> and <code>yDirection</code>.</li>
                    <li>Two private <code>Vector2</code> fields to store calculated offsets: <code>xVector</code> and <code>yVector</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Sub-Problem 1 (Read Input):</strong>
                Inside <code>Update()</code>, poll Unity's virtual axes:
                <ul>
                    <li>Query <code>Input.GetAxis("Horizontal")</code> and store the result in <code>xDirection</code>.</li>
                    <li>Query <code>Input.GetAxis("Vertical")</code> and store the result in <code>yDirection</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Sub-Problem 2 (Construct Vectors):</strong>
                Convert directional intent into physical displacement scaled by frame rate:
                <ul>
                    <li>Calculate <code>xVector</code> using <code>new Vector2(...)</code> with X scaled by <code>xDirection * xSpeed * Time.deltaTime</code> and Y set to <code>0</code>.</li>
                    <li>Calculate <code>yVector</code> using <code>new Vector2(...)</code> with X set to <code>0</code> and Y scaled by <code>yDirection * ySpeed * Time.deltaTime</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Sub-Problem 3 (Apply Translation):</strong>
                Call <code>transform.Translate()</code> for each vector to displace the player's position in world space.
            </li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save your script and return to the Unity Editor. Press Play and press WASD or the arrow keys.
        Your character should now glide smoothly in all four directions!
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Testing Assumptions</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if you delete <code>* Time.deltaTime</code> from your vector calculation? Why does the player suddenly blast off into deep space on the first keypress?</li>
            <li>In the Inspector for Player, change <code>xSpeed</code> to <code>0</code>. What happens when you press A and D versus W and S?</li>
            <li>What happens if you accidentally swap the vectors and pass <code>yVector</code> when moving horizontally?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Comparing Input Functions Live</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In <code>PlayerController.cs</code>, replace <code>Input.GetAxis("Horizontal")</code> with <code>Input.GetAxisRaw("Horizontal")</code>. Run the game. How does stopping feel compared to before?</li>
            <li>Press W and D simultaneously to move diagonally. Does moving diagonally feel slightly faster than moving straight in one cardinal direction? (Hint: In 2D math, what is the length of a hypotenuse with sides 1 and 1?)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>State the three sub-problems of character movement (input → vector calculation → translation)</li>
            <li>Explain the difference between <code>Input.GetAxis</code>, <code>Input.GetAxisRaw</code>, and <code>Input.GetKey</code></li>
            <li>Explain why multiplying by <code>Time.deltaTime</code> is necessary</li>
            <li>Locate and tune <code>xSpeed</code> and <code>ySpeed</code> in the Unity Inspector</li>
            <li>Commit your new movement code to your GitHub fork</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — input, vectors, and translation in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — input and vector functions</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Coming up next: <a href="1.5a.html">1.5a — Movement II: Speed, deltaTime &amp; Tuning</a></p>
</div>`;
